{
  "chapter": "langgraph/persistence",
  "tier": "build",
  "notes": [
    "A checkpoint is state and position written after each superstep. The unit is the node",
    "boundary, so on resume execution restarts at the beginning of the node.",
    "Nothing inside a node is memoized. A node that made three calls and crashed on the fourth",
    "starts again at the first, and the framework has no record that any of them occurred.",
    "Idempotency keys are what close that gap, and they must be derived deterministically from",
    "the thread. A random key generated inside the node changes on replay and defeats the",
    "whole mechanism.",
    "Human-in-the-loop has a double-execution problem: the interrupted call returns its",
    "recorded result, and everything else in that node runs again. One side-effecting call per",
    "node is the cheap structural fix.",
    "A read-only effect re-running costs a few cents and returns the same answer. That is the",
    "case where node-level replay is harmless.",
    "Moving to a durable store makes checkpoints survive a process. It does not make",
    "resumption coordinated or automatic: nothing notices a dead process, and a lease is yours",
    "to build.",
    "A crashed run nobody resumes is durable and permanently stopped, which looks identical to",
    "a run that finished.",
    "maxNodeEntries is a hard backstop on node entries. A correct run never approaches it; an",
    "implementation that forgets to memoise an interrupt would otherwise loop forever."
  ],
